from biology_course_maps import p,m
MAPS={3:[
m('00-inspector-calls-overview','An Inspector Calls overview',[],[
p('Act 1: power exposed','Setting: Brumley, 1912. Engagement celebration is interrupted by Goole.','Arthur: factory dismissal after a pay strike. Sheila: shop dismissal caused by jealousy.','Meaning: comfortable lives can depend on unequal power.'),
p('Act 2: appearances break','Gerald: protection and shelter coexist with an unequal affair. Sheila returns the ring.','Sybil: class prejudice blocks charitable help. She condemns the father before identifying Eric.','Method: disclosure and dramatic irony increase pressure.'),
p('Act 3: lesson tested','Eric: coercive conduct, pregnancy and stolen money. Eva refuses stolen funds.','Inspector: connects harm and social duties. Identity later becomes doubtful.','Sheila and Eric retain guilt. Others welcome relief. Final call renews investigation.'),
p('Characters and choices','Arthur and Sybil: status, confidence and resistance. Sheila and Eric: faults, remorse and learning.','Gerald: helpful actions and unequal control. Goole: moral investigation. Eva: absent, but morally active.','Judge each motive. Do not assume all harms are identical.'),
p('Shared responsibility','Class: employers, customers and charities control Eva’s options. Gender: economic and sexual vulnerability.','Generations: changed attitudes contrast with restored complacency. Reputation: respectability conceals harm.','Central question: is avoiding punishment the same as acting responsibly?'),
p('The ending reopens the case','Cycle: apparent escape is overturned. The lesson still concerns admitted conduct.','Audience: Goole’s identity remains uncertain. The ambiguity does not erase responsibility.')]),
m('01-characters-and-responsibility','Characters and responsibility',[],[
p('Arthur Birling','Role: manufacturer seeking business and status. Dismisses Eva after a strike.','Language: commercial priorities frame people through cost and profit.','Irony: wrong predictions weaken authority. Ending: welcomes possible escape.'),
p('Sybil Birling','Role: socially confident charity leader. Rejects Eva through prejudice.','Language: distancing class labels replace attention to individual need.','Irony: condemns the father before learning he is Eric. Status does not guarantee care.'),
p('Sheila Birling','Start: jealousy plus customer influence costs Eva a job.','Development: admits harm, questions Gerald and challenges comfortable appearances.','Ending: responsibility survives doubts about Goole. Growth does not erase guilt.'),
p('Eric Birling','Conduct: drinking, coercion and stolen money create serious harm.','Language: evasive descriptions reveal discomfort. Financial help does not excuse earlier conduct.','Ending: admits responsibility. He is flawed, not a simple moral hero.'),
p('Gerald Croft','Position: socially desirable fiancé. Helps Daisy but holds more power in the affair.','Development: confesses, then investigates Goole and shares the family’s relief.','Interpretation: recognise kindness and self-interest together.'),
p('Goole and Eva','Goole: directs questions, challenges evasions and broadens the moral message. Identity stays unresolved.','Eva: never appears. Others control the telling of her life.','Agency: seeks fair pay and refuses stolen money. Represents vulnerable ordinary people.')]),
m('02-themes-and-stagecraft','Themes and stagecraft',[],[
p('Responsibility and guilt','Pattern: separate choices combine to narrow Eva’s options.','Contrast: genuine acceptance versus fear of scandal. Remorse matters if values change.','Ending: technical doubts do not undo admitted conduct.'),
p('Class, money and gender','Power: Arthur controls work. Sheila influences a shop. Sybil controls aid.','Gerald and Eric: greater social and economic power affects relationships.','Contrast: secure dining room versus Eva’s insecurity outside it.'),
p('Generations and appearances','Sheila and Eric become more willing to learn. Their original faults remain significant.','Gerald complicates a simple age divide. Sybil also shows women can defend hierarchy.','Respectability: public roles conceal harmful behaviour.'),
p('Irony and revelation','1912 setting: audience knows Arthur’s confident predictions are wrong.','Sybil: condemns the unknown father before identifying her own son.','Ordered disclosures: suspense turns a mystery into a moral enquiry.'),
p('What the stage shows','Lighting: comfortable atmosphere becomes exposed. Single setting: pressure accumulates.','Doorbell: interrupts self-interest. Act endings: recognition and anticipated confession create suspense.','Goole: detective and morality-play features, without a proven supernatural identity.'),
p('A developed answer','Task: use the printed extract and the wider play. Track the character or idea across moments.','Evidence: short quotation or precise event. Method: explain meaning and stage effect.','Judgement: connect development to the question. Avoid retelling or unrelated history.')])
]}
MAPS[4]=[
m('00-christmas-carol-overview','A Christmas Carol overview',[],[
p('Stave 1: warning','Scrooge: rejects family and charity. Bob works in cold conditions. Marley warns him.','Evidence: “solitary as an oyster” suggests emotional enclosure. Chain: selfish choices become a burden.','Purpose: expose isolation and make change urgent.'),
p('Stave 2: remembering','Past: lonely school, Fan’s kindness, Fezziwig’s party, Belle’s departure.','“Another idol”: wealth replaces love through a religious metaphor.','Purpose: regret and recovered values. Explanation is not an excuse.'),
p('Stave 3: others matter','Present: Cratchit affection, Tiny Tim, Fred’s gathering, Ignorance and Want.','Children: allegory makes social neglect visible. Tim: possible loss challenges an abstract view of poverty.','Purpose: empathy and shared responsibility.'),
p('Stave 4: consequences','Silent future: indifference to a death, stolen possessions, family grief and Scrooge’s grave.','Contrast: money cannot buy loving remembrance. Future is conditional, not fixed.','Purpose: fear becomes urgency to act.'),
p('Stave 5: action proves change','Scrooge: sends food, gives to charity, visits Fred and raises Bob’s salary. Tiny Tim survives.','“A second father”: familial metaphor replaces isolation with care.','Purpose: redemption is sustained conduct, not a cheerful mood.'),
p('Why three spirits?','Sequence: each spirit teaches a different part of the moral lesson.','Connection: regret, empathy and urgency help turn knowledge into changed action.')]),
m('01-characters-and-relationships','Characters and relationships',[],[
p('Scrooge’s recovery','Start: money, fear and emotional enclosure. Past pain explains but does not excuse cruelty.','Change: defends Fezziwig, regrets Belle, cares about Tim and faces his grave.','End: salary, charity and family. “Light as a feather” suggests release from a burden.'),
p('Marley: a warning double','Similarity: another businessman who neglected human duties.','Chain: choices forge consequences. “Mankind was my business” redefines work through metaphor.','Difference: Scrooge still has time to change.'),
p('Christmas Past','Light: truth and painful memory. Changing age combines youth with recollection.','Scenes: kindness and loneliness reveal values Scrooge has forgotten.','Resistance: trying to extinguish the light suggests rejection of self-knowledge.'),
p('Christmas Present','Warmth and abundance: generous alternative to hoarding.','Cratchits: love has worth without wealth. Poverty still harms.','Ignorance and Want: social deprivation threatens the wider community.'),
p('Christmas Yet to Come','Silence: Scrooge must interpret the scenes. Darkness and solemn movement create dread.','Contrast: indifference to the rich man versus grief for Tim.','Question: can different choices change what may happen?'),
p('Family and lost kindness','Bob and Mrs Cratchit: care and anger reveal the costs of low wages. Tiny Tim: a valued individual.','Fred and Fan: invitations and affection offer belonging.','Fezziwig: humane employer. Belle: love displaced by wealth.')]),
m('02-themes-context-and-methods','Themes, context and methods',[],[
p('Poverty and responsibility','Poor people: individuals with dignity, not surplus numbers. Cratchit happiness does not erase deprivation.','Marley and children: neglected duties have personal and social consequences.','Ending: charity plus better wages makes change practical.'),
p('Family and Christmas','Shared rooms, meals and invitations: warmth and belonging. Scrooge: cold isolation.','Christmas: concentrates generosity, forgiveness and community.','Promise: keep its values throughout the year, not only one day.'),
p('Redemption and time','Past: causes and missed kindness. Present: duties. Future: possible consequences.','Future: conditional warning leaves room for choice.','Actions: the final stave confirms a lasting change.'),
p('Context linked to evidence','1843: industrial growth alongside deprivation. Dickens’s childhood work informs concern for vulnerability.','Poor Law and workhouses: harsh relief contrasts with personal compassion.','Population arguments: abstract numbers are challenged through Tiny Tim.'),
p('Form and narrative voice','Novella: concentrated moral journey. Ghost story: fear opens a route to change.','Staves: musical title suggests shared celebration. Ending revisits the opening world differently.','Narrator: humour, direct address and emotion shape judgement.'),
p('Imagery and essay writing','Cold/warmth: emotional contrast. Light/dark: insight and fear. Chains: consequences. Food: sharing and need.','Evidence: connect a short anchor with method and meaning. Track development.','Task: printed extract and wider novella, with relevant context woven into analysis.')])
]
MAPS[5]=[
m('00-unseen-poetry-overview','Unseen poetry overview',[],[
p('Understand the whole poem','Speaker: voice in the poem, not automatically the poet. Situation: what happens and where.','Overview: what is described and what deeper idea emerges?','Read: sentences across line endings. Check title, turns and ending.'),
p('Language and imagery','Words: precise connotations. Metaphor/simile: qualities transferred by comparison.','Personification: attitude towards a non-human subject. Sensory detail: imagined experience.','Sound/repetition: explain this effect, not an automatic meaning.'),
p('Structure and form','Structure: order, stanza shifts, contrasts and ending. Form: overall kind or shape.','Enjambment: continuing sentence. Caesura: internal pause. End-stopping: closure or pause at a line end.','Turn: show what changes. Free verse still has deliberate organisation.'),
p('Build a developed reading','Point: answer the task. Evidence: short and precise. Method: particular word, image or arrangement.','Meaning: explain the idea and its effect. Development: connect another moment.','Avoid: technique lists, unsupported guesses and invented biography.'),
p('Compare both poems','Shared idea: compare attitudes, images and development, not only matching subjects.','Both poems: evidence and analysis within linked paragraphs.','Difference: similar objects can express opposing values. Check each ending.'),
p('Manage the tasks','Single poem: 15 marks, about 20 minutes. Comparison: 25 marks, about 40 minutes.','Include reading and planning. Give the comparison its larger share.','Check: task focus, accurate evidence, methods and the overall meaning.')]),
m('01-analysis-and-comparison','Analysis and comparison',[],[
p('Literal and deeper meaning','Literal: what is directly described. Deeper: what the details imply about experience or values.','Waiting: cup and extra place reveal unresolved attachment.','Departure: cup and key begin a chosen movement towards independence.'),
p('Tone and viewpoint','Tone: attitude of the voice. Mood: atmosphere created. They may contrast.','Pronouns, questions and changes of certainty help establish viewpoint.','Ambiguity: support plausible readings. Do not turn guesses into facts.'),
p('Analyse a specific choice','Image: name the relevant qualities and connect them to the idea.','Example: bus windows carrying other lives place companionship beyond the waiting speaker.','Effect: public movement contrasts with private stillness.'),
p('Trace structure accurately','Opening: establishes expectation. Turn: changes time, place or thought. Ending: resolves or complicates.','Waiting: room, street, return indoors. Departure: house, journey, increasing distance.','Meaning: similar movement can reinforce attachment or freedom.'),
p('Different responses to absence','Waiting and Departure are the original practice poems in the notes and Q&A.','Comparison: the same general subject does not mean the same attitude.'),
p('An effective paragraph','Claim: a meaningful similarity or difference. Evidence: details from both poems.','Analysis: explain each method and meaning. Link: why the difference matters.','Response: informed interpretation with evidence, not only personal liking.')])
]
